===Noun===
{{en-noun|~}}

# {{lb|en|uncountable|also|figurative}} [[increase#Noun|Increase]] by [[natural#Adjective|natural]] [[growth]], especially the [[gradual#Adjective|gradual]] [[increase#Noun|increase]] of [[organic#Adjective|organic]] [[body#Noun|bodies]] by the [[internal#Adjective|internal]] [[addition]] of [[matter#Noun|matter]]; organic growth; also, the [[amount#Noun|amount]] of such growth. {{defdate|from early 17th c.}}
#: {{antonyms|en|nonaccretion}}
#* {{RQ:Chesnutt House Behind the Cedars|chapter=I|passage=There might have been a slight '''accretion''' of the moss and lichen on the shingled roof.}}
#* {{RQ:Wharton Age of Innocence|chapter=IV|passage=The immense '''accretion''' of flesh which had descended on her in middle life like a flood of lava on a doomed city had changed her from a plump active little woman with a neatly-turned foot and ankle into something as vast and august as a natural phenomenon.}}
# {{lb|en|uncountable}} (Gradual) increase by an [[external#Adjective|external]] addition of matter; {{lb|en|countable}} an [[instance#Noun|instance]] of this.
#: {{antonyms|en|nonaccretion}}
#: {{ux|en|A mineral augments not by growth, but by '''accretion'''.}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|author=w:Nathaniel Parker Willis|title=Death of Edgar Poe|work={{w|Town & Country (magazine)|Home Journal}}|date=October 20, 1849|url=http://www.eapoe.org/papers/MISC1827/18491020.htm|passage=Suddenly starting from a proposition, exactly and sharply defined, in terms of utmost simplicity and clearness, he rejected the forms of customary logic, and by a crystalline process of '''accretion''', built up his ocular demonstrations in forms of gloomiest and ghastliest grandeur, {{...|or in those of the most airy and delicious beauty, so minutely and distinctly, yet so rapidly, that the attention which was yielded to him was chained till it stood among his wonderful creations, till he himself dissolved the spell, and brought his hearers back to common and base existence, by vulgar fancies or exhibitions of the ignoblest passion.}}}}
#* {{quote-text|en|year=1855|author=w:George Cornewall Lewis|title=An Enquiry Into the Credibility of the Early Roman History|passage=To strip off all the subordinate parts of his as a later '''accretion'''}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1891|title=The Women of the French Salons|author=w:Amelia Gere Mason|url=https://www.gutenberg.org/files/2528/2528-h/2528-h.htm|passage=Our social life is largely a form, a whirl, a commercial relation, a display, a duty, the result of external '''accretion''', not of internal growth.}}
#* {{RQ:Hardy Tess|passage=She had no fear of the shadows; her sole idea seemed to be to shun mankind—or rather that cold '''accretion''' called the world, which, so terrible in the mass, is so unformidable, even pitiable, in its units.}}
#* {{RQ:London Burning Daylight|passage=Two-story log buildings, in the business part of town, brought him from forty to fifty thousand dollars apiece. These fresh '''accretions''' of capital were immediately invested in other ventures.}}
#* {{quote-web|en|url=https://www.theatlantic.com/technology/archive/2012/03/why-wikipedias-fans-shouldnt-gloat/254584/|title=Why Wikipedia's Fans Shouldn't Gloat|date=2012-03-16|author=Edward Tenner|work=The Atlantic|passage=Written by '''accretion''' rather than from a single author's interpretation Wikipedia has a neo-positivist mania for facts that devalues interpretation in depth, yet in matching Friedrich's review against Nabokov it also shows that it is far from neutral.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=2018|author=w:Shoshana Zuboff|chapter=12|title=The Age of Surveillance Capitalism|passage=The systematic '''accretion''' of violence and complicity that engulfed whole populations at extreme velocity invoked a kind of bewilderment that ended in paralysis, even for many of the greatest minds of the twentieth century.}}
## {{lb|en|geology}} Gradual increase of [[land#Noun|land]] by the [[deposition]] of [[waterborne]] [[sediment#Noun|sediment]].
##: {{antonyms|en|attrition|erosion}}
# {{lb|en|uncountable|also|figurative}} ''Followed by'' '''[[of#Preposition|of]]''': external addition of matter to a [[thing]] which [[cause#Verb|causes]] it to grow, especially in amount or [[size#Noun|size]].
# {{lb|en|uncountable}} The [[process#Noun|process]] of [[separate#Adjective|separate]] [[particle]]s [[aggregate#Verb|aggregating]] or [[coalesce|coalescing]] together; [[concretion]]; {{lb|en|countable}} a thing [[form#Verb|formed]] in this manner.
#: {{ux|en|the '''accretion''' of particles to form a solid mass}}
## {{lb|en|astrophysics}} The [[formation]] of [[planet]]s, [[star#Noun|stars]], and other [[celestial body|celestial bodies]] by the aggregating of matter [[draw#Verb|drawn]] together by [[gravity]]; also, the growth of a celestial body through this process.
##* {{quote-web|en|url=https://www.theatlantic.com/science/archive/2018/04/planets-are-built-from-pebbles/558854/|title=Earth May Have Been Formed by a Bunch of Tiny Space Pebbles|date=2018-04-26|quotee=Michiel Lambrechts|author=Alexandra Witze|work=The Atlantic|passage=“In many ways, pebble '''accretion''' is the most efficient way of adding mass to a body,” says Lambrechts.}}
# {{lb|en|countable|chiefly|figurative}} Something [[gradually]] [[add#Verb|added]] to or [[grow#Verb|growing]] on a thing [[externally]].
#: {{coi|en|an '''accretion''' of earth}}
## {{lb|en|[[conservation]] [[science#Noun|science]]}} A [[substance#Noun|substance]] which has [[build up#Verb|built up]] on the [[surface#Noun|surface]] of an [[object#Noun|object]], rather than [[become]] [[embed#Verb|embedded]] in it.
##: {{ux|en|'''Accretions''' of dirt on clothing are often left in place by conservators because they can provide additional details about the artefact’s importance or history.}}
# {{lb|en|law}}
## {{lb|en|uncountable}} Increase in [[property#Noun|property]] by the addition of other property to it (for example, [[gain#Noun|gain]] of land by {{l|en|alluvion|t=the deposition of sediment by a [[river#Noun|river]] or [[sea]]}} or {{l|en|dereliction|t=[[recession]] of [[water#Noun|water]] from the [[usual#Adjective|usual]] [[watermark#Noun|watermark]]}}, or [[entitlement]] to the [[product#Verb|products]] of the property such as [[interest#Noun|interest]] on [[money#Noun|money]]); or by the property [[owner]] [[acquire|acquiring]] another [[person#Noun|person]]'s [[ownership]] [[right#Noun|rights]]; [[accession#Noun|accession]]; {{lb|en|countable}} an instance of this.
## {{lb|en|uncountable}} Increase of an [[inheritance]] to an [[heir#Noun|heir]] or [[legatee]] due to the [[share#Noun|share]] of a [[coheir|co-heir]] or [[colegatee|co-legatee]] being added to it, because the latter person is [[legally]] [[unable#Adjective|unable]] to [[inherit]] the share.

